            <field name="html_content">
                &lt;h2&gt;Module 4: Crowd Management at Events and Incidents&lt;/h2&gt;
                &lt;p&gt;Crowd control is a public security practice to prevent stampedes, fights, riots, and dangerous congestion.&lt;/p&gt;

                &lt;h3&gt;Crowd Management Essentials&lt;/h3&gt;
                &lt;ol&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Signage reliance:&lt;/strong&gt; clear directional and security signs at key locations.&lt;/li&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Communication plan:&lt;/strong&gt; reliable radio/phone channels inside and outside venue.&lt;/li&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;One communication language:&lt;/strong&gt; standardized terms for all teams.&lt;/li&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Post-event review:&lt;/strong&gt; capture lessons learned and team observations.&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;/ol&gt;

                &lt;h3&gt;Crowd Density Awareness&lt;/h3&gt;
                &lt;ul&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;Crowd risk is measured by density, not only total size.&lt;/li&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;Static dense crowds can reach critical pressure quickly.&lt;/li&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;Dynamic moving crowds can turn unstable near bottlenecks.&lt;/li&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;Monitor choke points: gates, corridors, stairs, and transport exits.&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;/ul&gt;

                &lt;h3&gt;Practical Control Methods&lt;/h3&gt;
                &lt;ul&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;Planning and preparation before doors open.&lt;/li&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;Use verbal commands and calm tone to direct flow.&lt;/li&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;Apply teamwork with engineering, administrative, and behavioral support.&lt;/li&gt;
                    &lt;li&gt;Use modern technologies and organizational tools where available.&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;/ul&gt;
            </field>
        </record>

